WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.600
Authentication is the process of verifying who or what is requesting access.

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:07.540
A person might authenticate to a web application.

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:12.960
A service account might authenticate to an API. A device might authenticate to a network.

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:16.060
A workload might authenticate to a cloud service.

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:21.560
In each case, the system is trying to decide whether the claimed identity can be trusted enough to continue.

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:27.140
It helps to separate identification, authentication, authorization, and accounting.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:33.920
Identification is the claim, such as a username, account number, client identifier, or device identity.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:36.140
Authentication verifies that claim.

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:39.480
Authorization determines what the verified identity may do.

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:44.740
Accounting records activity for audit, billing, investigation, or operational visibility.

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:50.400
Confusing these ideas leads to designs where login success is mistaken for permission to do everything.

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:54.460
Authentication is foundational, but it is not sufficient by itself.

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:59.880
A system can authenticate the right user and still allow that user to access the wrong record.

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:04.260
It can authenticate a service and still give that service excessive privileges.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:09.280
It can authenticate successfully and then fail to protect the session that represents the user.

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:17.500
Good authentication supports access control, accountability, auditability, and trust, but it must work with the rest of the security design.

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:20.840
Failed authentication design affects more than security.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:28.500
It can create user frustration, support burden, operational outages, confusing incident response, and customer confidence problems.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:31.360
The goal is not simply to make login harder.

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:41.900
The goal is appropriate identity assurance: strong enough for the risk, usable enough for legitimate users, observable enough for operators, and maintainable over the account lifecycle.
